WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.120
I'm Geraldo Rivera, and you're about to witness a live television event.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.720
A massive, concrete vault has been discovered.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.920
Well tonight, for the first time, that vault is going to be open, live!

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:15.740
(Squidward): HOLY SHRIMP!

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:32.900
(MatPat): Hello, Internet!

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.220
Welcome to Film Theory,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.880
the show that uncovers sea crits [secrets] with all the determination and success of Plankton.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.060
(whispering): Aw, shoot, that's not really a good thing, is it?

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:47.260
Spongebob Squarepants as a franchise has been so omnipresent since its debut in 1999

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.920
that I think most of us assume that it would never go away.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.340
Which is part of the reason why the loss of Stephen Hillenburg, its creator, at age 57 by ALS was so tragic.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.600
It's not exaggerating in the slightest to say that he gave us some of the most iconic characters,

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.980
most quotable lines,

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:05.940
and memorable moments of recent history.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.460
He is, without question, one of the most influential people in children's programming

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.140
and current internet culture.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:18.580
It's probably for all those reasons that one of the most requested Film Theory topics of last year has been from Spongebob;

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:24.920
specifically the question that all us burger lovers, sponge lovers, and spongy-burger lovers wanna know--

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.560
what is the Krabby Patty's secret formula?

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.820
It's a question that's persisted for so long that it feels like it should be sacrilegious to try and answer it.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:38.760
But...if there's one thing that Film Theory stands for, it's treating nothing as sacred.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:45.060
So today, Spongebob is on the menu, and I consider it my honor to see if we can find out the secret formula

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.220
that tickles the taste buds of our imagination,

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.180
or if this secret will remain trapped in Bikini Bottom forever.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:56.460
The biggest problem with trying to figure out the Krabby Patty's secret formula is that anytime it comes up in the show,

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.980
we run into some suspicious problems.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.060
These problems make it hard to pinpoint just what the secret formula actually is.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.600
We know for a fact that the secret formula for a Krabby Patty is referring to the patty itself,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:13.580
and not what's actually on the burger, because we already know the full list of what goes on these things...

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.060
...or, do we?

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:21.520
The Krusty Krab is surprisingly fast and loose with this information since as early as season 1, episode 6--

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:22.580
the episode "Pickles"--

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.460
Spongebob reveals all the ingredients to a Krabby Patty with the works:

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:33.640
(Spongebob): I can't put a patty on a bun with lettuce, cheese, onions, tomatoes, ketchup, mustard, pickles and top bun together in that order!

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.420
(MatPat): From the mouth of the chef himself.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.300
But then the story changes, because two seasons later we hear a different recipe

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.120
from the Krusty Krab commercial shown in "As Seen On TV"--season 3, episode 7.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.880
(Mr. Krabs): We start with a fresh patty, grilled and juicy.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:53.980
(Mr. Krabs): Add some crisp undersea veggies and cheese, topped off with secret sauce and some buns.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.580
(Mr. Krabs): Voilà, a Krabby Patty!

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.580
(MatPat): It's pretty significantly different.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:03.340
This one is missing mustard, tomatoes and onions, which are not underseas veggies.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.940
I'll give him cucumbers since, I guess, those could be sea cucumbers.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:13.820
And this one has secret sauce, which Spongebob didn't list at all, despite him describing a Krabby Patty "with the works"--

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:16.220
literally everything on it.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.300
(TV Host): Can you make me one with everything?

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:20.740
(MatPat): Oh, and uh, by the way.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:25.080
At no point is it said in the show that you need a pinch of Poseidon powder,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.540
like some recent rumors circulating the Internet have been saying--that was a mistake on the fan wiki

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:32.580
and it has actually no grounding in the show itself.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.500
That's why you gotta be careful when trusting non-primary sources.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:39.480
Though, I will say "Poseidon powder" is slang for cocaine.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.480
And if cocaine were indeed the secret ingredient of these patties,

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.420
it would result in the addictive power and taste of love that the Krabby Patty often possesses.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:51.640
So, maybe it wasn't that far off--but like I said, no actual evidence of it in the show.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:58.080
Because, if there was evidence of it in the show, you know I would be all over that like Squidward on a vault full of Krabby Patties.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.560
Telling you your favorite cartoon characters are addicted to cocaine is, like, my favorite pastime.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.900
So, what is really going on here?

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.000
Well, for the answer maybe we can turn to the real recipe.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.280
Luckily, in "Krabby Road"--season 6, episode 1--Plankton dupes Spongebob into joining his band

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.380
which gives him a chance to steal the Krabby Patty secret formula,

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:25.360
and for the first and, to my knowledge, only time, we actually get a glimpse of it right before Plankton is caught.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.240
And what we see is...

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:27.820
(record scratch)

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:28.780
...gibberish?

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.660
What's the deal?

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.860
This is the official Krabby Patty secret formula kept in the restaurant,

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:36.220
so why is just a bunch of gobbledygook?

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.340
Well, two different possibilities exist:

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.400
Possibility 1: It's the real formula, but it's written down using some kind of coded cypher.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.700
And if Plankton had the right decryption key, he could translate that garbled gibberish into the real thing.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:55.360
Possibility 2: This ingredients list isn't real. Mr Krabs is lying about the recipe.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.940
And if you try to break apart this formula, this starts to seem more and more likely.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.840
One of the most common ways to break a simple cypher is with frequency analysis,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:10.680
the idea being that if you have a long enough string of text, you can match certain letters in the cypher to certain letters based on their frequency.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:16.920
For example, a letter that appears a lot of times in the cypher is more likely to be a vowel like "e" or "a"

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.840
or consonant like "s" or "t".

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:24.380
But when we run Spongebob's Krabby Patty formula through a frequency analysis tool, we find these results:

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.940
the most common letters are "h" and "g" followed by "s", "f" and "d".

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.660
The frequency analysis tool doesn't suggest any possible solutions to the cypher.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:38.280
g-d-s-g-e-h-s-g is pretty hard to map onto anything when it has so many repeating letters,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.340
let alone a word that would actually show up in an ingedients list.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:47.340
Plugging that into a cryptogram solver, the only words that could possibly translate into these letters are

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.280
"kickback", "gangling", "tastiest" and "elsewise".

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.360
None of these are Krabby Patty ingredients; you can't have 4 cups of "elsewise".

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:01.360
"Tastiest" looks promising, but if you try to use that cypher to solve the rest of the recipe you just get even more gibberish,

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.160
and none of the other words work out.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.540
But the letters and their frequency could still provide another hint.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.860
You see, they're all letters from the middle part of the keyboard

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.200
and the two most common letters are "h" and "g" specifically,

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.780
which are in the absolute dead center of the keyboard.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:22.520
What would fit perfectly with this arrangement of letters as if someone like Mr. Krabs just wanted Spongebob

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:28.100
to think that this was the secret recipe, so he just mashed on the keyboard to produce these non-words--

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.920
and here's where Mr. Krabs' real secret formula starts to come out.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.380
It's all a ruse. There is no secret formula.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:39.560
It's why the ingredients list for the Krabby Patty seems so variable throughout the series

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:41.960
both with and without the special sauce

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:45.520
why the official patty recipe is a bunch of keyboard mashing.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.920
It's here that we learn a very important lesson about Spongebob as well as our own world:

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.220
it's not about the food, it's about the business.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:57.380
Mr. Krabs is a lot of things, and one thing he is not is an expert chef.

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.420
But what he is, is an expert businessman.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:06.660
The secret to the Krusty Krab success isn't his amazing recipe, but his amazing marketing ability.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.660
He creates the mystery around his Patty as a one-of-a-kind burger.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.660
But lets face it, this is a hamburger...

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.560
Krabby burger...

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:14.700
whatever, it's not unique.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:18.180
The secret formula is all just a big marketing ploy.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.860
Doesn't seem likely? Just look at examples in the real world.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:26.960
Technically, there is nothing about a McDonalds burger you can't replicate at home, or at another restaurant.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.260
Comparing the Big Mac to the Big King, these things are esentially the same.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:35.160
Most of the value from McDonalds lies in the brand that people trust.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.420
To produce 100,000 burgers per day at over 30,000 locations around the globe,

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:43.940
all making it possible to go to almost any city in the world, see those Golden Arches and say:

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.260
"Yeah, I know what kind of experience I'm gonna have there.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.160
"I don't know if I'll like anything else around here, so I might as well get the good old reliable Big Mac

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.900
"instead of taking a chance at that Mom and Pop hoagie stand."

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:58.900
McDonalds' brand is reliability, the Krusty Krab's brand is uniqueness.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:04.480
It's the reason that McDonalds spends $2 billion a year on advertising.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:08.700
You could make the best food in the world, but if no one knows about it you're gonna go out of business pretty quickly.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:13.880
But if you're a savvy businessman like Mr. Krabs, or $137 billion company like McDonalds,

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:20.420
creating awareness for your brand is a lot more important than serving up patties that are objectively the best tasting.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.840
The Krabby Patty is the ultimate guerrilla marketing tactic:

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:27.960
you start a rumor about your own franchise that makes it sound vague and mysterious,

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:30.600
(Spongebob): Love it! Kind of vague and mysterious.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.740
(MatPat): and then, rather than spend money on marketing you let word of mouth do the work for you

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:38.220
and you get people talking about your product without having to spend a dime.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.440
(customers all talking in agreement)
(Customer): Well, that sounds very interesting!

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.400
(MatPat): Don't think Mr. Krabs' tactic worked?

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:48.540
Spongebob debuted in the 1990's and people are still talking about the Krabby Patty secret formula,

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.200
trying to uncover the elusive bit of knowledge in a fictional show,

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:55.540
a piece of knowledge that doesn't really exist!

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:58.940
For a real world example, consider the nameless juice brand.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:04.860
A brand of lemonade that refuses to actually put a brand name on itself and instead is just known for its mysterious flavor.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:09.180
The actual secret behind the fruity drink is that it contains some vegetable juice as well,

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:12.700
which makes it about as unique as V8--so basically not at all.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:18.060
That said, the mystery around that brand has driven massive underground word of mouth.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:23.300
Heck, even McDonalds' own secret sauce is just mayonnaise, pickles, mustard and a few spices.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:30.340
But the fact that they call it "secret" makes it seem like it's more exclusive, and therefore more in demand, than it really is.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:35.480
So both in real life and in 2000's cartoons, it's not about having the Krabby Patty secret formula,

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:39.580
it's just about having something you call a secret formula.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:45.240
And as a bonus in Spongebob, it distracts Mr. Krabs' main competitor by sending Plankton on a wild goose chase

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.880
for a formula that he's never actually gonna be able to find.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.100
The only thing that throws a monkey wrench into Mr. Krabs' plans, though, are his employees.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:57.700
Hence, he hires someone like Squidward, who doesn't really care about the secret, and Spongebob.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:03.720
Spongebob is honest to a fault, a real stand-up sponge, and he doesn't take kindly to things like fraud and deception.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:08.380
So if Spongebob knew about the Krabby Patty's lack of a secret formula, he might let that slip

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:12.600
or feel guilty about implicitly lying to everyone everytime he served up a burger.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:18.980
And so Mr. Krabs' solution? Slap his claws onto the keyboard for a minute and give Spongebob a fake formula.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.760
Make him think it's real, and don't worry if it ends up getting stolen.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:28.200
It sounds outlandish, or just a cop-out to say that there's no real formula and that there's nothing secret

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.500
to the Krabby Patty that makes it magically delicious.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:36.840
I mean, we all remember how Squidward completely lost his mind the first time he sunk his teeth into a Krabby Patty in just one bite.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:39.660
(Squidward): I love Krabby Patties!

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:45.300
(MatPat): Surely, there must be something at work here, and there is. It's just marketing, once again.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.560
According to Aaron Garvey,

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:50.460
an assistant professor of marketing at the
UK Gatton College of Business and Economics,

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:56.900
the way that people feel about a product is heavily linked to psychology and their brand association with that product.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:58.760
In his research, he found, quote:

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:02.360
"You could take the exact same product that you were thinking about taking to market,

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.300
"and you could put it in front of consumers,

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:06.520
"and you could change what price you were going to launch it at,

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:08.140
"and you could change what brand it was.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:11.440
"And they would have dramatically different impressions of that product.

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:14.720
"And they would tell you incredibly different things about the product.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:19.680
"That they thought it was made of different materials, that they thought it weighed more, or it weighed less." [END QUOTE]

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.820
So if Mr. Krabs says that the Krabby Patty's secret formula is what makes it so delicious,

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:25.660
he's actually not lying.

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:31.040
If he has created a brand association, created what is essentially a legendary burger,

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:33.720
people will experience it differently.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:37.960
If what people believe about a product's weight or materials is dependent on the brand they believe it is,

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:45.580
that effect is going to be just as strong, if not stronger, when it comes to totally subjective qualities, like how good a burger tastes.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:49.020
The Krabby Patty secret formula might have started as a lie,

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:53.480
but now the real secret ingredient is an ingredient that everyone thinks is there,

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.240
but one that doesn't truly exist.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:57.460
But, hey...!

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:00.260
